WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.590
If you were searching for a hidden gem buried
within the streaming slushpile, you could

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:09.809
do a lot worse than Lockout, a 2012 masterwork
of French science-fiction that's currently

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.790
burning up the charts at Netflix.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:16.350
When Netflix released their Top Ten list this
week, viewers were surprised to find the underappreciated

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.920
foreign film showing a strong 8th place.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.430
As of May 19th, Lockout breached the streamer's
most-viewed list, joining the ranks of hotly

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:28.840
anticipated Netflix originals like The Wrong
Missy and recognizable franchises like Despicable

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:29.840
Me.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.270
The popular streamer only recently lifted
up its digital curtain to make at least a

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.680
tiny sliver of their tightly held viewing
statistics public.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:41.450
Since earlier this year, they've been promoting
a weekly "Top Ten" of their most watched content,

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:45.829
albeit without revealing the actual numbers,
and the results have definitely been interesting.

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:50.399
So far, the rankings have been dominated by
a predictable mix of buzz-worthy original

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:54.079
content and licensed properties with broad
family appeal.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:57.960
Films like Lockout surprise us when they crack
the list, because they don't fit comfortably

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:58.960
in either box.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.940
So, what business does a little-known French
film from eight years ago have climbing the

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.010
rankings to such heights?

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:08.670
As it turns out, Lockout is actually a pretty
wild ride, and U.S. viewers who likely missed

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.870
its original release have been there for it.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:16.130
The 2012 action film is the directorial debut
of French filmmakers James Mather and Stephen

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:20.610
Saint Leger They hired Luc Besson of The Fifth
Element fame to assist with the screenplay,

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:25.230
though both Mather and Saint Leger are also
credited as writers on Lockout.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:29.230
For a first foray, the greenhorn directors
landed a pretty blockbuster cast.

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:34.230
Lockout stars Guy Pearce as protagonist Snow,
opposite Maggie Grace as Emilie Warnock.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:39.150
They're joined by Vincent Regan, Joseph Gilgun,
Lennie James, and Peter Stormare.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.909
Pearce's Snow is a man framed for a crime
he didn't commit in a far future world.

00:01:42.909 --> 00:01:46.750
He is offered the opportunity to earn his
freedom if he rescues the President's daughter

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:50.790
Emilie, played by Grace, from a notorious
orbital prison known as MS One.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:56.210
MS One has been taken over by a violent gang
of inmates in full revolt, and Emilie is currently

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:57.210
their captive.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.479
Gilgun and Regan play Alex and Hydell, the
inmate ringleaders who engineer the prison

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.920
riot and take Emilie as a bargaining chip.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.440
"Don't get me wrong, it's a dream vacation,
I mean, I load up, I go into space, I get

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.310
inside the maximum security nuthouse, save
the president's daughter…"

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:17.700
If you think some of these elements sound
familiar, legendary sci-fi director John Carpenter

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:18.480
agrees.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.040
"You go in, find the president, bring him
out in 24 hours and you're a free man."

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.280
"24 hours huh?"

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:25.580
"I'm making you an offer."

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:29.980
He successfully sued Mather and Saint Leger
for plagiarizing the story of his classic

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:34.900
genre films Escape from New York and Escape
from LA. Carpenter was eventually awarded

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:40.310
€20,000 for the infringement, in addition
to the €10,000 awarded to screenwriter Nick

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:43.870
Castle and the €50,000 awarded to MGM.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.670
An appeal of the decision was rejected back
in 2016, bringing the matter to a close.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:52.569
Reasonable minds can differ as to whether
the European courts ruled rightly in this

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:53.569
case.

00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:57.300
It should be noted that European intellectual
property law tends to be much more unforgiving

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.300
than U.S. law.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:02.460
What might qualify as an infringement in the
European system could be deemed a tribute

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.319
under the American paradigm.

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:08.060
If Mather and Saint Leger were going to look
to older works for inspiration, they could

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:10.250
do a lot worse than Carpenter's films.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:14.420
Whether you consider it a ripoff or a tribute,
the results on display in Lockout speak for

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:15.420
themselves.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:20.250
Lockout was originally released to 2,308 theaters
in the United States and Canada.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:26.060
Over its brief run, it only made $14 million
domestically, adding another $18 million to

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:27.060
that total, worldwide.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:30.670
"With that kind of attitude, I can see why
people don't like you."

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:34.159
"Oh come on, people love me."

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:38.519
Critical response was mixed at the time, though
most reviewers lauded Guy Pearce's performance

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:39.910
in the lead role as Snow.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.610
Derek Malcolm at the London Evening Standard
wrote that,

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:47.070
"It's laughably over the top but Pearce, a
decent actor, anchors it somewhere between

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.269
Escape From New York and The Transformers."

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:54.069
Some critics speculated at the time that Lockout
suffered from unflattering comparisons to

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:58.349
Carpenter's Snake Plissken movies, but others
saw merit in what Mather and Saint Leger were

00:03:58.349 --> 00:03:59.580
trying to accomplish.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.640
Sarah Gopaul at Digital Journal wrote,

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.319
"For a night of shameless entertainment and
explosions, Lockout is a winning choice."

00:04:06.319 --> 00:04:08.720
That seems to be exactly what Netflix viewers
are after.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.110
Eight years after the film's initial release,
it appears that streaming audiences have decided

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:17.930
that Lockout deserves a second look, and many
of them officially like what they see.

